# AEGIS-COGNITION Persistent Operating Rule

- Favor high-leverage performance work first: hot paths, allocations, lock contention, FFI boundaries, and batch workflows.
- Preserve and strengthen invariants; do not trade correctness for micro-optimizations.
- Prefer minimal, concrete code changes with clear validation steps.
- Keep benchmark-aware development as default when touching Rust or Python runtime paths.
- Keep workflow continuity first-class: every meaningful work session should end with an updated checkpoint artifact in `artifacts/`.
- When a session changes, resume from the latest checkpoint artifact and the planning document instead of re-deriving context.
- Make handoff state explicit: capture current status, last validated commands, changed files, and the next best action in the checkpoint.
- Keep imports, package layout, and benchmark harnesses consistent and runnable.
- Avoid unnecessary files; prefer lightweight rule and artifact updates over prose docs.
- If a change affects runtime behavior, validate with the smallest relevant build/test/bench command.
- For cross-session work, prefer checkpoint artifacts over chat-only memory so the next session can continue without reconstruction.
